/ Coxeter groups → every regular & semiregular polyhedron, one recipe
/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/ A Coxeter group is generated by reflections in a handful of mirrors
/ that meet at angles π/m.  In 3D a *linear* diagram is all it takes —
/ three mirrors, described by just two integers p,q:
/
/            o───p───o───q───o           mirror1 ∠ mirror2 = π/p
/         mirror1  mirror2  mirror3       mirror2 ∠ mirror3 = π/q
/                                         mirror1 ∠ mirror3 = π/2
/
/ Changing those two integers changes the symmetry of space itself:
/
/     [3,3] → A₃   order 24    tetrahedral   (rotations A₄)
/     [4,3] → B₃   order 48    octahedral    (rotations S₄)
/     [5,3] → H₃   order 120   icosahedral   (rotations A₅)
/
/ Wythoff's construction then turns a group into a SOLID: drop a seed
/ point into the fundamental triangle bounded by the three mirrors and
/ reflect it everywhere.  The seed's position — three "ring weights"
/ (t₀ t₁ t₂), how far it sits from each mirror — selects the polyhedron:
/
/     1 0 0  regular {p,q}         0 1 0  rectified
/     0 0 1  dual    {q,p}         1 1 0  truncated {p,q}
/     1 0 1  cantellated           0 1 1  truncated {q,p}
/     1 1 1  omnitruncated  (a seed off every mirror → orbit = |G|)
/
/ So (p, q; t₀ t₁ t₂) is a five-integer ADDRESS for a uniform polyhedron,
/ and this ONE method reaches every Platonic solid AND every Archimedean
/ solid in these families.  The golden-ratio coordinates and ±1 cubes of
/ the other constructions are just special seeds of this kaleidoscope.
/
/ The engine — mirrors, bfc closure, group, orbit, wythoff, adj/faces —
/ lives in lib/geometry.k (also used by demo/earth.k to build its globe).
/ This file is the showcase: group orders, orbit–stabiliser, a V/E/F census.
/
/ Run: ink demo/geometry.k

2: "lib/geometry.k"

/ ── run: group orders + orbit–stabiliser ─────────────────────────────
`0 0: "reflection groups from the diagram [p,q]:"
grp: {[nm;p;q]
  G: group mirrors[p;q]
  `0 0: nm,"  |G|=",($#G),"  rotations=",$+/0<{det x}'G}
grp["A3 [3,3] tetrahedral";3;3]
grp["B3 [4,3] octahedral ";4;3]
grp["H3 [5,3] icosahedral";5;3]
`0 0: ""
`0 0: "orbit-stabiliser  |orbit| = |G| / |stab|  (H3, |G|=120):"
`0 0: "  seed on 2 mirrors 1 0 0 → icosa-face stab 6 → ",($#wythoff[5;3;1. 0. 0.])," verts"
`0 0: "  seed on 2 mirrors 0 0 1 → icosa-vert stab 10 → ",($#wythoff[5;3;0. 0. 1.])," verts"
`0 0: "  seed on 1 mirror  0 1 0 → edge stab 4 → ",($#wythoff[5;3;0. 1. 0.])," verts"
`0 0: "  seed off all      1 1 1 → trivial stab 1 → ",($#wythoff[5;3;1. 1. 1.])," verts = |G|"

/ ── census: name, (p,q,ring), V, E, F, Euler ─────────────────────────
census: {[nm;p;q;t]
  V: wythoff[p;q;t]
  e: # edges adj V
  f: 2+e-#V
  ring: ,/$_ t
  `0 0: nm," [",($p),",",($q),"] ",ring,"  V=",($#V)," E=",($e)," F=",($f)," χ=",$((#V)+f)-e}

`0 0: ""
`0 0: "the five Platonic solids — pure ring choices:"
census["tetrahedron   ";3;3;1. 0. 0.]
census["cube          ";4;3;1. 0. 0.]
census["octahedron    ";4;3;0. 0. 1.]
census["dodecahedron  ";5;3;1. 0. 0.]
census["icosahedron   ";5;3;0. 0. 1.]

`0 0: ""
`0 0: "…and 'other shapes': rectify or truncate the SAME mirrors →"
`0 0: "the Archimedean solids, no new geometry, just a different seed:"
census["cuboctahedron ";4;3;0. 1. 0.]       / rectified cube
census["truncated cube";4;3;1. 1. 0.]
census["trunc. octahed";4;3;0. 1. 1.]
census["rhombicubocta ";4;3;1. 0. 1.]
census["trunc.cubocta ";4;3;1. 1. 1.]       / omnitruncated  (V = |B3| = 48)
census["icosidodecahed";5;3;0. 1. 0.]       / rectified dodecahedron
census["trunc. dodecah";5;3;1. 1. 0.]
census["trunc. icosah.";5;3;0. 1. 1.]       / the football / soccer ball
census["rhombicosidod ";5;3;1. 0. 1.]
census["trunc.icosidod";5;3;1. 1. 1.]       / omnitruncated  (V = |H3| = 120)
census["trunc.tetrahed";3;3;1. 1. 0.]
